Consolidated Recommendations for Strengthening the PDF Extraction Vocabulary
Coverage assessment

Yes, with the additions below, this document consolidates the material raised throughout this conversation and the statement families reviewed so far. It covers the terminology, document fields, table structures and parsing behaviours identified across BNZ, SBS, RaboDirect, TSB, ASB, Kiwibank, Westpac, American Express and ANZ transaction, credit-card and loan documents.

However, it would be inaccurate to claim that a static word list can cover every possible future statement. New banks, historical forms, generated reports, OCR defects and product-specific documents will introduce new wording. The right target is:

Complete coverage of all supplied examples, supported by a structure that allows unfamiliar wording to be added safely without changing extraction code.

The earlier recommendations remain valid. The following changes should be incorporated into the consolidated document based on Amex, ANZ - 9 Columns (4) and ANZ Loan (5).

1. Retain the two-file design, but tighten the responsibility of each file

The two-file design remains appropriate:

labels.yaml should identify labelled document-level values.
lexicon.yaml should identify tables, columns, row types, direction notation, transaction classes, structural markers and parsing patterns.

The files should not become two overlapping bags of banking terminology.

In particular:

Balance labels, due dates and account identifiers belong in labels.yaml.
Date, description, withdrawal, deposit and balance column headings belong in lexicon.yaml.
Payment, fee, interest, refund and transfer wording belongs in transaction-class vocabularies.
CR, DR, OD and column position establish direction.
Totals, carry-forwards and section headings require dedicated structural categories.

This separation is essential because the same word can perform different roles. For example, interest can appear as a percentage-bearing account attribute, a loan transaction, a credit-card charge or a summary total.

Recommendations for labels.yaml
2. Complete the account and party identification model

The shared dictionary should support distinct fields for:

account_name
account_number
account_type
card_number
customer_number
membership_number
loan_number
facility_number
statement_number
primary_cardholder
prepared_for

American Express uses Prepared for, Membership Number and Card Number as separate identifiers. Its card numbers use a masked pattern such as XXXX-XXXXXX-13008, which does not match a conventional 16-digit card pattern.

Add synonyms including:

membership number
member number
membership no
prepared for
card number
card no
account number
account no
loan number
home loan number
facility number
customer number
customer no
client number

The account and card regexes should not determine the semantic field alone. The nearby label and document type should establish whether an identifier is an account, card, membership, loan or customer number.

3. Complete the balance model

The recommended balance fields are:

opening_balance
closing_balance
current_balance
available_balance
available_credit
principal_balance
amount_payable
interest_owing
overdue_amount
over_limit_amount
frozen_amount
uncleared_amount
authorisations_amount
Add amount_payable

American Express separately displays Closing Balance and Amount Payable. These may have the same value in a particular statement, but they are distinct concepts and should not be collapsed during extraction.

Recommended synonyms:

amount payable
payment
payment amount
amount due
total amount due
total due

A standalone payment label should only be accepted when immediately associated with a monetary value in a payment-summary region.

Add principal_balance

The ANZ loan transaction table uses Principal Balance, not merely Balance.

Recommended synonyms:

principal balance
outstanding principal
loan principal
principal outstanding
outstanding loan balance
Add interest_owing

The ANZ loan summary provides Interest Owing as at [date].

Recommended synonyms:

interest owing
interest owing as at
accrued interest
unpaid interest
interest outstanding

If possible, preserve the associated “as at” date separately as interest_owing_date.

4. Expand period and statement-date handling

The supplied documents now demonstrate:

explicitly labelled statement periods;
inline “from date to date” periods;
START - date periods;
statement opening and closing dates;
“as at” dates;
loan-summary periods; and
documents containing multiple consecutive statements.

ANZ includes a first period expressed as START - 30 Sep 2019, rather than two complete dates.

The date-range model should therefore support:

full date to full date;
full date through full date;
START - date;
start to date;
an explicitly labelled opening date and closing date;
an unlabelled range immediately following a recognised statement-period heading.

For START - date, the start should remain null or be represented as an open interval. It should not be set equal to the first transaction date unless the application explicitly records that as an inference.

