7. Distinguish statement data from attached notices

ANZ Loan (5) contains both loan statements and separate notices concerning fixed-interest expiry and rate changes. The notices include fields such as Loan Number, Current Balance, Current Fixed Interest Rate, New Floating Interest Rate, Next Payment Date and Current Maturity Date.

The parser should classify pages or sections as:

loan statement;
rate-expiry notice;
interest-rate change notice;
informational continuation page.

Values extracted from a rate-change notice should not silently overwrite statement-period values. Each value should retain:

source page;
document-section type;
effective date, where printed;
original label;
confidence.
Recommendations for lexicon.yaml
8. Expand table-header vocabulary with the new exact phrases

Add the following header phrases:

transaction type and details
principal balance
foreign spending
amount $
details
withdrawals
deposits
fee description
amount
opening date
closing date

ANZ uses a combined Transaction type and details column. ANZ loan statements use Date, Description, Withdrawals, Deposits, Principal Balance. American Express uses Details, Foreign Spending, Amount $.

Whole phrases should receive more weight than component terms. For example:

transaction type and details is stronger evidence than either type or details;
principal balance is stronger than balance;
foreign spending is stronger than foreign or spending.
9. Support physically expanded multi-column descriptions

The ANZ document is described as a nine-column layout, although several of those physical columns form one conceptual Transaction type and details field. A row may contain:

transaction code;
account number or merchant;
particulars;
code;
reference;
serial or terminal identifier;
withdrawal;
deposit;
balance.

The extractor should not assume that each visual column corresponds to one final output field. It should support:

transaction_type
description
particulars
code
reference
serial_number
withdrawal
deposit
balance

and then allow unused middle columns to be concatenated into a lossless raw description.

The recommended output should retain:

description_raw
transaction_type
other_party
particulars
code
reference
serial_number

This avoids losing content when the statement presents semi-structured details across several visual columns.

10. Add transaction-code dictionaries

The ANZ statements provide an explicit code legend:

AP: Automatic Payment
BP: Bill Payment
DC: Direct Credit
ED: Electronic Dishonour
FX: Foreign Exchange
IP: International EFTPOS Transaction
IF: International Payment
AT: Automatic Teller Machine
CQ: Cheque/Withdrawal
DD: Direct Debit
EP: EFTPOS Transaction
IA: International Money Machine
VT: Visa Transaction.

These should be stored in a dedicated category such as transaction_type_codes, not in debit_markers or credit_markers.

The code alone does not always establish direction. For example, the statement layout and the populated Withdrawals or Deposits column provide stronger evidence. The code should assist classification and field segmentation.

Recommended structure conceptually:

code
normalised transaction class
description
possible direction
direction confidence

DR and CR should remain direction markers. DD, DC, BP, AP, EP and similar abbreviations are transaction types.

11. Add American Express grouping and subtotal markers

American Express groups transactions under:

New Transactions for [name]
Other Account Transactions
Total of new transactions for [name]
Total of other account transactions.

Add structural categories for:

Group headings
new transactions for
other account transactions
transactions for
charges for
card transactions for
Group totals
total of new transactions for
total of other account transactions
total transactions for
subtotal for

The parser should:

identify the group heading;
capture the associated cardholder or card number as group context;
apply that context to following rows;
stop applying it at the next group heading;
exclude the subtotal row from transaction output.

A name following New Transactions for is group metadata, not part of the next merchant description.

12. Support American Express single-amount direction

American Express uses a single Amount $ column. Credits are indicated with CR, including payment and credit-adjustment rows.

This reinforces the previously recommended direction hierarchy:

explicit CR or DR;
separate debit or credit column;
signed or parenthesised amount;
validated balance movement;
transaction-class hint;
unresolved if direction remains ambiguous.

Add:

CR
CREDIT
CREDIT ADJUSTMENT
CREDIT BALANCE TRANSFERRED

to appropriate direction or transaction-class categories.

However, Credit Adjustment and Credit Balance Transferred should be semantic credit hints, while CR is explicit direction notation.

