ANZ explicitly uses Totals at end of page, Totals at end of period, Balance brought forward from previous page and No transactions for this period.

No transactions for this period should produce a valid empty statement result containing account metadata and balances. It should not be treated as an extraction failure.

18. Correct the meaning of “available credit”

ANZ transaction-account statements state Your available credit is [amount] as at the closing date of this statement, even though the product is a Business Current Account.

Therefore:

do not assume available_credit only occurs on credit-card statements;
preserve the printed label exactly;
map it to available_credit, not automatically to available_balance;
allow product context to determine interpretation downstream.

This is an important example of why semantic normalisation must not overwrite source meaning.

19. Expand masked identifier recognition

Support at least:

standard NZ bank accounts;
extended NZ accounts;
masked 16-digit cards;
American Express-style masked identifiers;
contiguous payment account numbers;
loan identifiers with an additional suffix.

Examples in the supplied documents include:

standard hyphenated bank accounts;
0222-0088120097-1004 loan identifiers;
XXXX-XXXXXX-13008 American Express identifiers.

The parser should preserve:

raw identifier;
normalised identifier;
masking status;
identifier type;
nearby label.

Do not run arithmetic validation or check-digit logic against masked identifiers.

